# 7. SCREEN TWO — PROGRAMME HIERARCHY EXPLORER

This is one of the most important parts of the entire redesign.

The existing hierarchical structure must be represented clearly and professionally.

There are four canonical root programmes:

1. Technology Programme
2. Design & Graphics Programme
3. Music Programme
4. Academics Programme

The existing hierarchy also defines these predefined sub-programme relationships:

- Smart Techies Programme under Technology Programme.
- Digital Literacy Programme under Technology Programme.
- Graphic Design Programme under Design & Graphics Programme.
- WAEC Preparation Track under Academics Programme.
- JAMB Preparation Track under Academics Programme.

These are predefined hierarchy concepts, not confirmation that every corresponding record currently exists in Firestore. Do not automatically insert them as live records.

### Recommended layout

Use a two-panel workspace.

**Left panel: Hierarchy Tree**

Display the programme roots and their nested children as an expandable tree.

The tree must support:

- Expand and collapse individual branches.
- Expand all.
- Collapse all.
- Search within the hierarchy.
- Select a node.
- Visually distinguish root programmes from children.
- Display child counts when available.
- Show node types.
- Indicate published, draft, or archived status.
- Clearly show the selected programme.

Use indentation, connecting lines, restrained folder/node icons, and typography to communicate hierarchy.

Do not make every tree node look like a separate card.

**Right panel: Selected Programme Inspector**

When an administrator selects a node, show its details in a structured inspector.

Include:

- Programme name.
- Description.
- Parent programme.
- Root programme.
- Breadcrumb path.
- Category.
- Node type.
- Delivery format.
- Target audience.
- Pricing information.
- Publication status.
- Direct enrolment setting.
- Whether it can contain children.
- Required or optional status.
- Progression rule.
- Prerequisites.
- Child programmes.

Use appropriate tabs or grouped information sections such as:

- Overview.
- Child Programmes.
- Details.
- Progression.
- Actions.

### Hierarchy actions

Provide accessible actions for:

- Add Root Programme.
- Add Child Programme.
- Edit Programme.
- Rename.
- Move Programme.
- Reorder.
- Publish.
- Unpublish or save as draft.
- Archive.
- Restore archived programme.
- View details.

These actions should be available through a clean contextual menu or well-organised action area.

Avoid putting too many buttons beside every node.

### Node types

The existing system supports three node types:

**CONTAINER**

A structural folder that organises other programmes. It is not directly enrolable.

**ENROLLABLE**

An actual learning unit that can be enrolled in.

**HYBRID**

A programme that can support direct enrolment while also containing nested child programmes.

Display these distinctions clearly with small labels or badges.

Do not use colour alone to communicate the differences.

### Progression

The hierarchy supports:

- Sequential progression.
- Flexible progression.
- Prerequisites.
- Required or optional programmes.

Make these relationships understandable without cluttering the tree.

Where useful, show a simple progression visualisation or relationship panel.

